Installing CGTK 1.4
===================

No configure script is shipped. Generate one first, which needs
autoconf, automake, libtool, gettext's autopoint and pkg-config (on
Debian, the autoconf, automake, libtool, autopoint and pkgconf
packages; autopoint is not pulled in by gettext):

  autoreconf -vif
  ./configure
  make
  make check          (optional: the programs that check themselves)
  make install        (as root, for the default prefix of /usr/local)

What it needs
-------------

  - a C99 compiler, POSIX threads and dlopen
  - FreeType 2 and fontconfig, which draw and find all text
  - libpng 1.6 or later, which reads PNG files
  - for the X11 backend: libX11, and libXext and libXrandr if found
  - for the Wayland backend: wayland-client, wayland-cursor, xkbcommon,
    wayland-protocols 1.20 or later and wayland-scanner
  - for GtkGLArea: EGL, and wayland-egl with the Wayland backend
  - awk, to make GTK+'s type tables and marshallers

At least one backend has to be found.


GLib, GModule, GThread, GDK and GTK+ are built and installed together,
and with them GtkGLArea, the OpenGL widget, where EGL is found.
Run "./configure --help" for the options; the ones most likely to be
wanted are:

  --prefix=DIR                  install under DIR [/usr/local]
  --enable-x11-backend          build the X11 backend [auto]
  --enable-wayland-backend      build the Wayland backend [auto]
  --enable-gl                   build GtkGLArea, which needs EGL, and
                                wayland-egl with the Wayland backend [auto]
  --disable-xrandr              do not use libXrandr, by which the X11
                                backend knows how often a screen is
                                refreshed [used if found]
  --with-default-theme=NAME     theme used when no gtkrc picks one [Altus]
  --disable-compat-pc           do not install the GTK+ 1.2 pkg-config
                                names (glib, gtk+, ...) as aliases


OpenGL
------

GtkGLArea is a library of its own, libgtkgl-1.4, so that nothing in
GDK or GTK+ depends on GL. A program that uses it includes
<gtkgl/gtkglarea.h>, and is built with

  gtk-config --cflags gtkgl      or   pkg-config --cflags gtkgl-1.4
  gtk-config --libs gtkgl        or   pkg-config --libs gtkgl-1.4

to which it adds the library OpenGL itself is in: on current systems
that is OpenGL (pkg-config's "opengl"), which is GL and nothing of a
window system; "gl" where there is only the older one. The
AM_PATH_GTKGL macro of gtkgl.m4 does both. examples/glarea has
programs to start from, one of them drawing without pause from the
main loop as an emulator or a game would; gtkgl/gdkgl.h says where the
widget differs from the GtkGLArea of GTK+ 1.2 and how a swap is timed.


Finding the libraries at run time
---------------------------------

With glibc, the dynamic linker finds libraries in directories such as
/usr/local/lib through its cache, /etc/ld.so.cache, so a newly
installed library cannot be loaded until "ldconfig" has been run.
"make install" and "make uninstall" run it when installing directly
(no DESTDIR) as root on a native glibc system. Otherwise, run it by
hand after installing:

  ldconfig

To skip it, give "make install LDCONFIG=". Package builds that install
with DESTDIR are left to run it themselves, as packages usually do.

If the prefix is one the dynamic linker does not search (for example
/opt/cgtk or a directory under $HOME), add its lib directory to
/etc/ld.so.conf.d/ and run ldconfig, or set LD_LIBRARY_PATH. Building
programs against such a prefix also needs its pkg-config directory:

  PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/opt/cgtk/lib/pkgconfig ./configure

musl keeps no cache and searches /usr/local/lib by default, so none of
this is needed there for the default prefix.
